<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Comalcalco</h1><p class="st-meta">600 CE – 900 CE (approx.) · capital Comalcalco</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The westernmost major Classic Maya city, in the Tabasco plain, unique for being built of fired brick rather than stone.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Comalcalco grew in the alluvial lowlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabasco">Tabasco</a>, close to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-coast-of-mexico">Gulf Coast</a>, where there was no building stone. It became a significant centre from about 600 CE Its ancient name was Joy Chan, and in 649 it was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortuguero">Tortuguero</a>, whose kings used the same <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/palenque">B'aakal</a> emblem as Palenque.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Builders made fired clay bricks, many incised or stamped with drawings, glyphs and marks, and used lime from burnt oyster shells for mortar. The city's acropolis held palaces and a tomb decorated with stucco figures. Its economy rested on fertile plains well suited to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a>, a crop that is still important in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabasco">Tabasco</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Comalcalco declined around 900 CE during the wider collapse of lowland Maya kingdoms. Its brick architecture remains unique in the Maya world.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/comalcalco.jpg" alt="Comalcalco" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Alfonsobouchot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Comalcalco.Iglesia_y_Palacio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 CE – 900 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Comalcalco</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maya religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chontal Maya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Joy Chan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=750&amp;focus=comalcalco" aria-label="Comalcalco on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2153 1500 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Comalcalco lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2473 1756h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2473 1756h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=750&amp;focus=comalcalco">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

